  "summary": "A printing technique that simulates continuous-tone imagery through varying sizes of dots arranged in a rosette pattern.",
  "description": "Created by varying the size of cyan, magenta, yellow, and black dots in a specific angle to create the illusion of continuous tone. Developed by Benjamin Day for newspaper printing, it became fundamental to commercial printing and photomechanical reproduction.",
  "culturalContext": "Halftone rosettes — the CMYK dot lattice where four screens meet at different angles — are the invisible architecture of print color: misaligned angles cause moiré, perfect angles create the rosette pattern visible under a loupe, and dot gain determines the whole gamut. Newspaper-era offset made rosettes the texture of mass media; risograph and zine revivalists celebrate the dot, and \"visible halftone\" is now a deliberate aesthetic from comic art to editorial design.",
